          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How can I tell from the tabular list that a code is a combination code rather than a plain one?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages don't give a clear rule for spotting a combination code in the Tabular List. They do describe what combination codes do, and a few examples.\n\nA combination code names two conditions, or a condition and its cause or complication, in one code. The coding guidance says that \"combination codes fold pairs of conditions together\" (ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10 summary). Examples include the diabetes family, where \"the combination code names the association, and the additional code carries the detail\" (Coding type 2 diabetes: E11), and the hypertension family, where hypertension with kidney disease moves to I12 and needs a second code for the stage of kidney disease (Coding hypertension: I10).\n\nThe passages also say that the Tabular List is the authority, because it carries the instructional notes that the Alphabetic Index leaves out. The \"Excludes1\" note is one example (ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions). Some codes are reported with a second code, such as the asthma code that a COPD entry tells you to add (Coding asthma: J45). A note like that is the kind of signal to look for, but the passages do not list the full set of note wording that marks a combination code.\n\nThe passages do not say which title wording or note patterns reliably identify a combination code. For that, check the full Tabular List entry and its instructional notes, or ask a trained coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
